---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/focus.css";
import { courseMeta } from "../../course-config";
import { href } from "../../lib/links";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

// Weights, names and dates come from the assessments collection so this page
// and /calendar/ and each brief cannot drift apart.
const assessments = (await getCollection("assessments")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.due.getTime() - b.data.due.getTime(),
);
const studios = await getCollection("sessions");
const lectures = await getCollection("lectures");
const totalWeight = assessments.reduce((t, a) => t + a.data.weight, 0);
const due = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-AU", {
  day: "numeric", month: "long", timeZone: "Australia/Canberra",
});

const outcomes = [
  { id: "LO1", text: "Trace each claim a waiting interface makes — a percentage, a time, a word, a motion — to the event in the underlying process that could support it, and mark the ones that have no possible source." },
  { id: "LO2", text: "Implement a file-upload waiting interface that behaves correctly under known duration, unknown duration, stall, timeout, disconnection, cancellation and retry, using a local simulator." },
  { id: "LO3", text: "Audit and repair a waiting interface's keyboard path, status text and reduced-motion behaviour against a stated checklist, recording failures as failures." },
  { id: "LO4", text: "Justify a design decision from evidence you actually gathered, and state a conclusion no stronger than that evidence supports — including deciding, with reasons, not to change anything." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Course overview"
  description="What the course studies, who it is for, what you make, and how the twelve weeks are put together."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <h2>What it studies</h2>
  <p>
    One widget. A file-upload waiting interface, and the question of what it is
    entitled to say while the upload is in flight.
  </p>
  <p>
    That sounds small and it is. The narrowness is the design: a progress bar is
    where a system's uncertainty about itself becomes something a user reads, and
    a semester is about the right length to work out how much of what a typical
    one says is supportable. The course spends twelve weeks moving one interface
    from <em>two states and a shimmer</em> to something whose every displayed
    number can be traced to an event.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Second-year students who can write HTML and CSS, and enough JavaScript to
    write a function that takes a callback and to reason about a promise. That is
    the whole prerequisite.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not need a background in psychology, statistics, or human-computer
    interaction. Where the course touches published work on perceived duration it
    links the work and says what it measured; where it has no evidence it says so.
    You are not expected to arrive with an opinion about design.
  </p>

  <h2>What you make</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone starts from the same deliberately broken prototype, <code
      >upload-ui</code
    >: two states, one of which is a lie, and a bar that sits at 99% for eleven
    seconds before failing silently. You keep the same repository for twelve
    weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    By week 12 it handles known and unknown durations, names its own denominator
    and estimator on screen, survives a stall, a timeout and a disconnection with
    messages that say what happened to the file, keeps written guarantees for
    cancel and retry, works from the keyboard and with motion off, and ships with
    a register of every claim left on its screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every transfer is simulated locally. No server is involved at any point, which
    is what lets you reproduce a stall on demand — the thing you can never do with
    a real network, and the reason the subject is teachable at all. You can try
    the same mechanism on the <a href={href("/simulator/")}>progress simulator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning outcomes</h2>
  <p>At the end of the course you can:</p>
  <dl class="outcomes">
    {outcomes.map((outcome) => (
      <>
        <dt>{outcome.id}</dt>
        <dd>{outcome.text}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p>
    Every week page names the outcomes it teaches, and every assessment names the
    outcomes it assesses. Nothing is assessed that a studio dated before the
    deadline has not taught — that is checked mechanically rather than promised.
  </p>

  <h2>How the semester runs</h2>
  <p>
    {studios.length} studios, {lectures.length} lectures, one artefact.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Studios</strong> — Thursdays, 14:00&ndash;17:00, every week. Three
      hours at the machine on the same prototype.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lectures</strong> — Tuesdays, 11:00&ndash;12:00, in weeks 1, 4, 7
      and 11 only. Four, not twelve. Each carries something you could not arrive
      at at a keyboard, and the studio two days later applies it. The other eight
      weeks open with a framing and a set reading in the studio itself, named on
      each week's page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Between studios</strong> — two to three hours. Each week page ends
      with a <em>Before next week</em> section that says what and how long.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you miss a week, each studio has a stage checkpoint (<code
      >git checkout stage-07</code
    >) that restores the mechanism the next week needs and leaves every judgement
    in it unmade. A floor, not an answer: you can rejoin without having finished
    the week before.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessment</h2>
  <table class="assessments">
    <caption>Three pieces, totalling {totalWeight}%</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Assessment</th>
        <th scope="col">Weight</th>
        <th scope="col">Due</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {assessments.map((a) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href={href(`/assessments/${a.id}/`)}>{a.data.title}</a></th>
          <td>{a.data.weight}%</td>
          <td>{due.format(a.data.due)}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    There is no examination. Each piece assesses what came before it and nothing
    that comes after, and the third is largely assembled from work already done in
    weeks 10 and 11.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Calendar" href="/calendar/">
      <p>Every teaching date and deadline in one table.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Studios" href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, each listed by the question it answers.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Readings" href="/readings/">
      <p>Everything the course sets, and why each week sets it.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Glossary" href="/glossary/">
      <p>The terms this course narrows, and what it narrows them to.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Progress simulator" href="/simulator/">
      <p>The five behaviours the course spends the semester on, in one page.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies and support" href="/policies/">
      <p>Extensions, integrity, and the week 11 consent arrangements.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .outcomes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 0.4rem 1rem;
    margin-block: 1rem 2rem;
  }

  .outcomes dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .outcomes dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .assessments {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-block: 1rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .assessments caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding-block-end: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .assessments th,
  .assessments td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.75rem 0.55rem 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-border, currentColor);
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .outcomes {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.1rem;
    }

    .outcomes dd {
      margin-block-end: 0.9rem;
    }
  }
</style>
